{
  "requirement": "A `{req}` opening fence MAY contain four or more colons. Habeas MUST accept opening fences of any length of at least three colons. A colon-only line with fewer colons than the opener MUST remain inside the requirement body, allowing a nested colon directive. A closing fence outside a fenced code example with at least as many colons as the opener MUST end the body.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Implementation**\n\n- `colon_fence` (src/lib.rs:651) is marked `{impl}`. It recognises a line of at least three leading colons, with up to three spaces of indentation. It returns the colon count and the remainder of the line. This accepts opening fences of any length of three or more.\n- `definition_blocks` (src/lib.rs:511) is marked `{impl}`. It records the opener's colon count as `fence_length`. It then searches for the first later line that meets all of these conditions:\n  - It is not inside a Markdown code block. The code ranges come from the pulldown-cmark parser, so a `:::` inside a fenced code example is ignored.\n  - It is a colon fence with `count >= fence_length`.\n  - It has nothing after the colons.\n- A colon-only line with fewer colons than the opener fails the `count >= fence_length` test. It stays in the body, so a nested `:::{note}` ... `:::` inside a `::::{req}` is kept.\n- A longer or equal closer outside code ends the body.\n- The other consumers (evidence validation, the preprocessor, terms) all go through `definition_blocks`. `src/index.rs` uses `colon_fence` for the `{requirements-index}` directive, which this requirement does not cover.\n- I found no unrelated behaviour in the marked code beyond the other requirements it is also marked for.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `a_longer_colon_fence_contains_a_shorter_nested_directive` (src/lib.rs:1107) checks a `::::` opener with a nested `:::{note}` block. It asserts the exact body span, the closed flag, and that the following text is excluded.\n- `validates_four_or_more_colon_fences_and_longer_closers` (src/evidence.rs:1763) checks the opener/closer pairs `::::`/`::::`, `::::`/`:::::` and `:::::::`/`::::::::`. Each has a nested `:::` directive in the body and text after the closer. It asserts that evidence validation sees the full body with no errors.\n- `colon_requirement_span_includes_markdown_and_excludes_following_text` (src/evidence.rs:1698) checks that a `:::` inside a fenced code example does not close the body.\n- Three-colon fences are covered by many other tests. The unclosed-fence case is covered by `reports_a_missing_closing_colon_fence`.\n\nThe tests cover the interesting cases: opener lengths, closer lengths, nested shorter fences and closers inside code. The implementation is fully marked and matches the requirement text.",
